<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-1-5-sequencing">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Sequencing</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>In this lesson, we will talk about algorithms using sequential steps or flowcharts and 
      start coding in Python with <c>print</c> statements and Turtle code to draw shapes like below.
    </p>

    <image source="_static/Unit1images/turtlePath.png"  width="80%" alt="Turtle drawing a path"/>

  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="sequencing">
    <title>Sequencing in Written Steps and Flowcharts</title>

    <idx>algorithm</idx>
    <idx>sequencing</idx>
    <idx>flowchart</idx>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.A.1</title>
      <p>Many tasks can be completed by following step-by-step instructions. An <term>algorithm</term>
    describes these steps in an order that can be followed to solve a problem.</p>
    </note>

    <p><term>
    Algorithms</term> are step by step instructions for solving a problem or completing a task.
    Before writing code, programmers often design algorithms to plan out the steps needed to solve a
    problem. Algorithms are used in many areas of life, not just in computer science. For example, a
    recipe is an algorithm for cooking a meal. A set of directions to a friend’s house is an
    algorithm for getting there. </p>

    <p>Watch the following video about algorithms by Dr. Ralph
    Morelli, one of the founders of Mobile CSP (<url
        href="https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1XfwXHnhzIxQQppKgxS6PAhc9f61LHeLcILd6feAHc8E/edit?usp=sharing">
    slides</url>, <url href="https://www.teachertube.com/videos/359066">TeacherTube version</url> ). </p>

    <video
      youtube="60CzIn2FIcM" label="video-algorithms" />


    <activity label="pbj-algorithm">
      <statement>
        <p>Write an algorithm in the form of written steps for how to make a peanut butter and jelly
    sandwich.
          Have a friend attempt to follow it.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </activity>

    <p>Watch the following video of how instructions to make a peanut
    butter and jelly sandwich
      can go horribly wrong if the instructions are not precise enough!</p>
    <video
      youtube="A9LDjdB5Uko" label="video-pbj" />

    <note>
      <title>1.5.A.2</title>
      <p>Algorithms can be represented in ways people can read and follow, such as written steps or <term>
    flowcharts</term>.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.A.3</title>
      <p>When representing an algorithm in written steps or a flowchart, each step should be clear
    and specific so the process can be followed correctly.</p>
    </note>

   <p>Algorithms can be written as text in any language or in <term>pseudocode</term> which is a way to describe 
    precise coding steps using a language like English or using <term>flowcharts</term>. 
    Here's an example of a flowchart for choosing a pet:</p> 
    
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/pet-flowchart.png"  width="80%" alt="Choosing a pet flowchart"/>

    <activity label="flowchart-clothes">
      <statement>
        <p>Create a flowchart to help someone decide what clothes to wear based on the weather.
          Be sure to include yes/no questions and different paths for at least 4 different outcomes.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.A.4</title>
      <p><term>Sequencing</term> means the steps of an algorithm are carried out in a specific
    order, one step at a time. Changing the order of the steps can change what happens or whether
    the task is completed correctly.</p>
    </note>

    <p>The activity below demonstrated that the steps
    of an algorithm need to be in order.
      Put the steps for sending a text message in order. Try them out of order and it will not work!</p>
  <activity
      label="algorithm-text-message" adaptive="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Put the following pseudocode algorithm steps for sending a text message
          in order. Click on Check Me to see if you are right.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block>
          <cline>Unlock your phone.</cline>
        </block>
        <block>
          <cline>Open the messaging app.</cline>
        </block>
        <block>
          <cline>Choose a contact.</cline>
        </block>
        <block>
          <cline>Type your message.</cline>
        </block>
        <block>
          <cline>Press Send.</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="print">
    <title>Python Output with <c>print()</c></title>
      <idx>print</idx>
      <idx>output</idx>

    <p>The <c>print()</c> function is used to display output to the screen in Python. Inside the
      parenthesis of the function, you can put in any message in quotes to be printed out, for
      example:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
        print("Hello World!")
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Try running this code in the exercise below. Notice that every Python command to the computer
      must be on a separate line.
    </p>

    <activity label="print1">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code. Then, change "World" to your name and run the code again.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          print("Hello World!")
          print("Good bye!")
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              self.assertIn("Hello",self.getOutput(),"Says hello")
              self.assertNotIn("World",self.getOutput(),"Change World to your name")

          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>


    <activity label="printMixedUp" adaptive="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Drag or click on the blocks you need to move them from the top section
          into the yellow area to create a print statement that will print “Hi!”.
          There are extra blocks that you don’t need.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <blocks layout="horizontal">
        <block>
          <c>print</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>(</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>"Hi!"</c>
        </block>
        <block>
          <c>)</c>
        </block>
        <block correct="no">
          <c>;</c>
        </block>
        <block correct="no">
          <c>Print</c>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>


  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="sequencing-print">
    <title>Sequencing in Code</title>
      <idx>IDE</idx>

    <p>In this e-book, you can write Python code right in the Active Code exercises. When you click
      on run, the code is <term>executed</term> or run by the computer, which means the computer
      follows the algorithm instructions in the code. </p>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.1</title>
      <p>When an app runs, a computer executes algorithms that are expressed as program code.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.2</title>
      <p>Programmers write code using tools such as <term>text editors</term> or <term>integrated
        development environments (IDEs)</term> that help them write and run programs. Some tools
        include AI features that can generate or autocomplete code, which programmers can adapt and
        test as they develop programs.</p>
    </note>

    <p>The <term>IDE</term> in this e-book is in the Active Code exercise windows, but you could
      copy the code into a <term>text editor</term> or <term>IDE</term> like VSCode that can be
      downloaded to your computer and run. </p>


    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.3</title>
      <p>Clarity and readability are important when expressing an algorithm in a programming
        language so others can understand and modify the code.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.4</title>
      <p>Sequential statements execute in the order they appear in the program, and changing the
        order of statements can change the program’s behavior or what the user sees.</p>
    </note>

    <p>To demonstrate that statements need to be in order, put the following Knock Knock joke in
      order:</p>
    <activity label="parsons-knock-knock" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show"
      language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Put the lines of this knock-knock joke in the correct order.
          Remember that Python executes statements sequentially in order from top to bottom.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block>
          <cline>print("Knock knock!")</cline>
        </block>

        <block>
          <cline>print("Who's there?")</cline>
        </block>

        <block>
          <cline>print("Lettuce.")</cline>
        </block>

        <block>
          <cline>print("Lettuce who?")</cline>
        </block>

        <block>
          <cline>print("Let us in, it's cold out here!")</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

    <p>Now let's try it in Python code. Python executes each line of code in order, so if the lines
      are out of order, the joke will not make sense when it is printed out. Put them in order by
      moving the lines of code, and then run it again to see the joke printed out correctly!
    </p>
    <activity label="activecode-knock-knock">
      <statement>
        <p> The lines of this knock-knock joke are out of order. Run to see what it prints out! Highlight and drag
          or cut and paste with (ctrl-x and ctrl-v) the <c>print</c> statements to put them in order, so the joke makes sense, and
          then run again. </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          print("Lettuce who?")
          print("Knock knock!")
          print("Let us in, it's cold out here!")
          print("Who's there?")
          print("Lettuce.")
        </code>

        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):

            def test1(self):
              output = self.getOutput()

              expected = "Knock knock!\nWho's there?\nLettuce.\nLettuce who?\nLet us in, it's cold out here!\n"
              
              self.assertEqual(output,expected,"The joke lines are in the correct order.")

          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="debugging">
    <title>Debugging</title>
      <idx>debugging</idx>
      <idx>syntax error</idx>
    <p>
      <term>Syntax errors</term> are bugs or mistakes in the way the code is written. For example,
      the following code has a syntax error because the quotes and parenthesis are not closed. Try
      running the code and see what happens. Try fixing the errors to <term>debug</term> the code. </p>
    <activity label="debug1">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code. Fix the errors and run again.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          print("Hello World!
        </code>
        <tests>
            from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
            class myTests(TestCaseGui):
              def test1(self):
                self.assertIn("Hello World!",self.getOutput(),"output")
            myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="turtles">
    <title>Python Turtles</title>
          <idx>turtle</idx>

     <p>Python has a Turtle library where turtle objects can move around on the screen and draw
      pictures. We can import the library and set up the drawing space and the turtle with the
      following code where # indicates a comment that is not executed but explains the code.
    </p>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
          import turtle               # use the turtle library
          yertle = turtle.Turtle()    # create a turtle named yertle
          yertle.shape("turtle")      # make yertle look like a turtle (default is an arrow)
        </code>
      </program>
    <p>We can set up a Turtle object like yertle that can be used to call functions to move the turtle around the screen. 
      For example, the following code will move the turtle forward 100 pixels, turn it left 90 degrees, 
      and move it forward 100 pixels again. The amount of movement or turning is put in parentheses 
      after the function name. </p>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
          yertle.forward(100)         # move forward 100 pixels
          yertle.left(90)             # turn left 90 degrees
          yertle.forward(100)         # move forward 100 pixels
        </code>
      </program>
  <p> 
    Try running the code to see how the turtle moves. You can change the numbers in the parentheses to move 
    or turn different amounts. You can also use other functions like <c>right()</c> to turn right, 
    <c>backward()</c> to move backward. Note that the turtle always starts facing to the right and moves forward
    whichever way it is facing. 
</p>
 <activity label="turtle1">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the code below. Then, add one more statement at the end to move yertle forward 100 pixels. Run again.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          import turtle               # use the turtle library
          yertle = turtle.Turtle()    # create a turtle named yertle
          yertle.shape("turtle")      # make yertle look like a turtle

          yertle.forward(100)         
          yertle.left(90)             
          yertle.forward(100)         
          # Can you add code below to make yertle move forward again?

        </code>
       <tests>
            from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
            class myTests(TestCaseGui):
              def test1(self):
                  input_count = self.getEditorText().count(".forward")
                  self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 3, "at least 3 forward statements")
            myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

    <activity label="turtle-capital-L" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
      <p>The following program uses a turtle to draw a capital L as shown below, but the lines are mixed up. 
        The turtle should turn to face south, draw a line that is 150 pixels long, 
        then turn to face east, and draw a line that is 75 pixels long. We have added a compass to 
        the picture to indicate the directions north, south, west, and east. 
        Drag the needed blocks of statements from the left column to the right column and put them in the 
        right order. There may be additional blocks that are not needed in a correct solution. 
        Then click on Check to see if you are right. 
        You will be told if any of the lines are in the wrong order or are the wrong blocks.</p>
  
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/TurtleL4.png" width="30%" alt="Line-drawn capital L with compass"/>
  
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
       <block order="7">
        <cline>import turtle</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
        <cline>ella = turtle.Turtle()</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="4">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>ella.right(90)</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>ella.turn(90)</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>ella.forward(150)</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <cline>ella.left(90)</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="1">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>ella.forward(75)</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>ella.go(75)</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>

   <activity label="turtle-square">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code. Can you make the turtle complete the square? Can you change its pen color 
          for each side of the square?
     </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          import turtle               
          tina = turtle.Turtle() 
          tina.shape("turtle") 
          tina.color("green")       # set pen color to green  
          tina.fillcolor("green")   # set turtle body and fill color to green

          tina.forward(100)         
          tina.right(90)          
          tina.forward(100)
          # add code to complete the square 


        </code>
       <tests>
            from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
            class myTests(TestCaseGui):
              def test1(self):
                  input_count = self.getEditorText().count(".forward")
                  self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 4, "at least 4 forward statements")
                  input_count = self.getEditorText().count(".right")
                  self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 4, "at least 4 right turns")
                  input_count = self.getEditorText().count(".color")
                  self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 2, "at least 2 colors")
                  
            myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="tracing">
    <title>Tracing Sequential Code</title>
      <idx>tracing</idx>
      <idx>trace table</idx>
   
    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.5</title>
      <p>Tracing program execution means following which statements are executed and what values are
        produced and stored as the program runs.</p>
    </note>

    <p>Pretend that you are the computer and trace through the code below line by line to figure out 
      what the turtle will draw.</p>
     
    <activity label="turtle-trace">
    <statement>
      <p>What shape will the program below draw when run?
      </p>
      <p> <fillin answer="square" mode="string" /></p>
      <pre>
import turtle    
zari = turtle.Turtle()    
zari.left(90)      
zari.forward(100) 
zari.left(90)                
zari.forward(100)       
zari.left(90)          
zari.forward(100)      
zari.left(90)          
zari.forward(100)       
</pre>
    </statement>
     <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test>
            <strcmp use-answer="yes" />
            <feedback>
              Correct!
            </feedback>
          </test>

          <test>
            <!-- else -->
            <feedback>
              <p>Try again. Choose between an A, C, L, circle, triangle, square, or pentagon.</p> 
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation> 
  </activity>


    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.6</title>
      <p>Programmers can trace execution by using debuggers or print statements to display values at
        different points in a program. When the following code runs, the program displays the value
        of expression followed by a new line. <c>print(expression)</c></p>
    </note>

    <p>The <c>xcor()</c> turtle command will print out the current x-coordinate of the turtle on the screen. 
    Try running the following program that uses <c>print</c> statements to trace the current location of the turtle.
    </p>
    
      <activity label="turtle-xcor">
      <statement>
        <p>Run the following code. Add another print statement to print out tina's current x-coordinate at the end. </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          import turtle               
          tina = turtle.Turtle() 
          tina.shape("turtle") 
          tina.color("purple")   
          tina.fillcolor("purple")    

          print( tina.xcor() )
          tina.forward(50) 
          print( tina.xcor() )                 
          tina.forward(50)
          # add another print statement 


        </code>
       <tests>
            from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
            class myTests(TestCaseGui):
              def test1(self):
                  input_count = self.getEditorText().count(".xcor")
                  self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 3, "at least 3 xcor")
                  self.assertIn("100",self.getOutput(),"50+50")
            myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>
    
    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.7</title>
      <p>The result of sequential statements can be determined by tracing code in order and keeping
        track of the values and output.</p>
    </note>

     <p>
      Trace tables can be used to track the values as they change
      throughout a program. To trace through code, write down a variable in each
      column or row in a table and keep track of its value throughout the
      program. Some trace tables also keep track of the output like below.
    </p>

      <image source="_static/Unit1images/traceTable.png" width="30%" alt="Trace table" />
    

       <activity label="turtle-trace2">
    <statement>
      <p>What x-coordinate will be printed out by this code?
      </p>
      <p><fillin answer="50" mode="string" /></p>
      <pre>
import turtle    
teeny = turtle.Turtle()       
teeny.forward(20)                 
teeny.forward(30) 
teeny.left(30)      
print( teeny.xcor() )     
</pre>
    </statement>
     <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test>
            <strcmp use-answer="yes" />
            <feedback>
              Correct! 
            </feedback>
          </test>

          <test>
            <!-- else -->
            <feedback>
            <p>Try again. Turning does not change the x-coordinate.</p>
            </feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
  </activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.5.B.8</title>
      <p>Programmers trace sequential code to confirm an app works as intended and the information
        it displays is correct and understandable.</p>
    </note>

  </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="turtle-project">
      <title>Turtle Project</title>

      <activity label="turtle-project">
      <statement>
        <p>Draw something interesting with the turtle. The code below also shows how to change colors, 
          fill in colors, lift up the pen, and draw dots. 
          You could draw a flower, a house for the turtle, a smiley face, or anything you want.
        </p>
   </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          import turtle               
          tina = turtle.Turtle() 
          tina.shape("turtle") 
          
          tina.color("blue")       # set pen color to blue    
          tina.fillcolor("green")  # set fill color to green     
          tina.begin_fill()        # start filling in the shape 
          # draw a closed shape
          tina.forward(100)         
          tina.right(90)
          tina.forward(100)
          tina.right(90)
          tina.forward(100)
          tina.right(90)
          tina.forward(100)
          tina.end_fill()         # end filling in the shape 

          tina.penup()            # put pen up so it doesn't draw
          tina.forward(150)       # move without drawing
          tina.pendown()          # put pen down so it draws

          tina.dot(50, "red")   # draw a red dot of size 20
          

        </code>
       <tests>
            from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
            class myTests(TestCaseGui):
              def test1(self):
                  input_count = self.getEditorText().count(".forward")
                  self.assertGreaterEqual(input_count, 8, "at least 8 forward statements")
            myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

     <activity label="turtle-project-reflection">
      <title>Project Reflection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Describe the purpose of your program above in 1-2 sentences.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </activity>

    </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-1-5">
    <title>Review Learning Objectives</title>

    <p>After this lesson, you should be able to:</p>
    <remark>
      <title>1.5.A</title>
      <p>Represent an algorithm that uses sequencing using written steps or a flowchart.</p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>1.5.B</title>
      <p>Develop code that runs sequentially and determine the result of sequential statements.</p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

